From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lagz0ne <15101444+lagz0ne@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 05:09:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 15/20] perf(canvas): a zoom step restyles the canvas only when
 its chrome has drifted

Chrome drawn inside the world at a fixed screen size (frame names, rings,
grips, pins) undoes the camera's zoom through the --zoom custom property
on the stage. The property is inherited, so every write restyles each
element under the stage and re-lays out and repaints whatever reads it.
Traced on a 188-frame page at 60-95% with 12 live documents in view, a
zoom in small steps as a pinch makes them: every one of 19 steps restyled
1555 elements (10-13 ms each on a loaded machine), re-laid out 390 and
repainted every frame name, where a pan step is one transform.

- The stage's --zoom now follows a moving camera in steps: it is rewritten
  once the zoom has drifted 10% from the value last written
  (chromeZoomStale, layoutZoom.ts) and exactly when the camera has rested
  for 120 ms. In between, that chrome is off its screen size by at most
  10%; a wheel notch or a zoom jump is past the drift and is written at
  once.
- The label layer, which places the size badge from the zoom, gets the
  exact zoom on every step: its own few elements are all that restyles.
  It carries data-stage-labels, where a script can read the camera's zoom.
- useCanvasDrawing read the zoom at all times for the pen's snap radius.
  The hook runs inside the Stage, so the whole Stage re-rendered on every
  zoom step (and its layout effect forced the restyle above from script).
  It now reads the zoom only while a path is being drawn.

Editor main thread, perf/bench.mjs "zoom 60>95>60%", four interleaved
runs against the previous build on a loaded machine (load 10-25), median:
1015 -> 516 ms, style 187-471 -> 93-195 ms, slow frames 5 -> 1.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
